# Geoms: layer_geoms

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])
```

## About this notebook

ggplot2's reference page for
[`layer_geoms`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/layer_geoms.html) has no examples, so this
notebook says only what the page describes and where Giggly stands.

A ggplot2 layer has two main parts: its geom, which decides how the data is displayed, and its
stat, which decides what is displayed. A layer's geom can be given in five ways: by the geom's own
function, such as `geom_area()`; as a stat's default; by name, as a string given to a stat; as the
geom's ggproto object; or to `layer()` itself. A geom turns the layer's data into graphical
primitives, such as points, lines, rectangles and polygons, styled by aesthetics such as colour,
fill and linewidth. Many geoms abstract over others, as a ribbon is a special polygon and an area a
special ribbon, and some compose several, as a boxplot is lines, rectangles and points. Giggly adds
a layer with a function for each geom: `Giggly.Plot.geom_point/2`, `Giggly.Plot.geom_bar/3`, which
counts with ggplot2's `stat_count()`, and `Giggly.Plot.geom_col/3`. Every other geom, giving a stat
a geom by name, `layer()` and `ggproto()` are not in Giggly yet.
